  "rawCandidate": "```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"selectedContextPaths\": [\n    \"README.md\",\n    \"config/app.json\",\n    \"src/greeting.py\",\n    \"src/main.py\"\n  ],\n  \"plan\": {\n    \"mutationTargets\": [],\n    \"requestedCheckIds\": []\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"kind\": \"read_only\",\n    \"files\": [],\n    \"citations\": [\n      \"README.md\",\n      \"config/app.json\",\n      \"src/greeting.py\",\n      \"src/main.py\"\n    ],\n    \"findingIds\": [],\n    \"summary\": \"The configured audience reaches stdout through a four-step chain. README.md documents the entry point and configuration location: 'Configuration lives in `config/app.json`; execution begins in `src/main.py`.' config/app.json supplies the value, containing the field \\\"audience\\\": \\\"traveler\\\". src/main.py is the entry point that reads that file, extracts the audience, and prints the result: it does `json.loads(Path(\\\"config/app.json\\\").read_text())[\\\"audience\\\"]` and passes it to `greeting(...)`, with the whole call wrapped in `print(...)` so the string is written to stdout. src/greeting.py defines the greeting behavior: `def greeting(audience: str) -> str: return f\\\"Welcome, {audience}.\\\"`, which formats the audience into the final message. The end-to-end output is therefore `Welcome, traveler.`\"\n  }\n}\n```",
